# Tracing `new Chalk({level: 3}).red("foo")` vs `new Chalk({level: 0}).red("foo")`

## 1. Where the level value gates escape-code emission (`source/index.js`)

Every styled call ultimately funnels through `applyStyle(self, string)` in `source/index.js`:

```js
const applyStyle = (self, string) => {
	if (self.level <= 0 || !string) {
		return self[IS_EMPTY] ? '' : string;
	}

	let styler = self[STYLER];

	if (styler === undefined) {
		return string;
	}

	const {openAll, closeAll} = styler;
	...
	return openAll + string + closeAll;
};
```

This is the single gate. `self.level` is read via the `level` getter on `proto`:

```js
level: {
	enumerable: true,
	get() {
		return this[GENERATOR].level;
	},
	...
}
```

which points back to the `Chalk` instance's `.level` property, set in `applyOptions`:

```js
const applyOptions = (object, options = {}) => {
	...
	const colorLevel = stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0;
	object.level = options.level === undefined ? colorLevel : options.level;
};
```

- With `new Chalk({level: 3})`, `object.level = 3`, so `self.level <= 0` is `false` in `applyStyle`, and the function falls through to `return openAll + string + closeAll;` — the ANSI open/close codes wrap the string.
- With `new Chalk({level: 0})`, `object.level = 0`, so `self.level <= 0` is `true`, and `applyStyle` returns `self[IS_EMPTY] ? '' : string;`. Since `.red('foo')` is not the `.visible` builder, `IS_EMPTY` is falsy, so it returns `string` unchanged — i.e. `"foo"` with no escape codes at all. Critically, the styler (open/close codes) is never even consulted at level 0.

## 2. How `levelMapping` picks the color model

`source/index.js` defines:

```js
// `supportsColor.level` → `ansiStyles.color[name]` mapping
const levelMapping = [
	'ansi',
	'ansi',
	'ansi256',
	'ansi16m',
];
```

This array is indexed directly by the numeric `level` (0–3) and only matters for the **RGB/hex/ansi256-derived** model getters (`rgb`, `hex`, `ansi256`, and their `bg*` counterparts), not for named colors like `.red`. In the `usedModels` loop:

```js
const usedModels = ['rgb', 'hex', 'ansi256'];

for (const model of usedModels) {
	styles[model] = {
		get() {
			const {level} = this;
			return function (...arguments_) {
				const styler = createStyler(getModelAnsi(model, levelMapping[level], 'color', ...arguments_), ansiStyles.color.close, this[STYLER]);
				return createBuilder(this, styler, this[IS_EMPTY]);
			};
		},
	};
	...
}
```

`levelMapping[level]` converts the numeric level to a model name string (`'ansi'`, `'ansi256'`, or `'ansi16m'`) that is then passed into `getModelAnsi`:

```js
const getModelAnsi = (model, level, type, ...arguments_) => {
	if (model === 'rgb') {
		if (level === 'ansi16m') {
			return ansiStyles[type].ansi16m(...arguments_);
		}
		if (level === 'ansi256') {
			return ansiStyles[type].ansi256(ansiStyles.rgbToAnsi256(...arguments_));
		}
		return ansiStyles[type].ansi(ansiStyles.rgbToAnsi(...arguments_));
	}
	if (model === 'hex') {
		return getModelAnsi('rgb', level, type, ...ansiStyles.hexToRgb(...arguments_));
	}
	return ansiStyles[type][model](...arguments_);
};
```

So `levelMapping[1] === 'ansi'` and `levelMapping[0] === 'ansi'` (both level 0 and level 1 downgrade to the basic 16-color ANSI codes if reached), `levelMapping[2] === 'ansi256'`, `levelMapping[3] === 'ansi16m'` selects true-color codes. (Note: for `.red`, `.green`, etc., this mapping array is irrelevant — those are plain named colors built directly from `ansiStyles.color.red` in the `for (const [styleName, style] of Object.entries(ansiStyles))` loop, which doesn't consult `levelMapping` at all; the mapping only governs the dynamically-computed `rgb`/`hex`/`ansi256` families.)

## 3. Where the per-level ANSI code generators live (`source/vendor/ansi-styles/index.js`)

The raw wrapper functions that actually produce escape-sequence strings for each level are defined at the top of the file:

```js
const ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET = 10;

const wrapAnsi16 = (offset = 0) => code => `[${code + offset}m`;

const wrapAnsi256 = (offset = 0) => code => `[${38 + offset};5;${code}m`;

const wrapAnsi16m = (offset = 0) => (red, green, blue) => `[${38 + offset};2;${red};${green};${blue}m`;
```

Inside `assembleStyles()`, these are attached to `styles.color` and `styles.bgColor`:

```js
styles.color.ansi = wrapAnsi16();
styles.color.ansi256 = wrapAnsi256();
styles.color.ansi16m = wrapAnsi16m();
styles.bgColor.ansi = wrapAnsi16(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
styles.bgColor.ansi256 = wrapAnsi256(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
styles.bgColor.ansi16m = wrapAnsi16m(ANSI_BACKGROUND_OFFSET);
```

These are exactly the functions `getModelAnsi` in `source/index.js` calls (`ansiStyles[type].ansi16m(...)`, `ansiStyles[type].ansi256(...)`, `ansiStyles[type].ansi(...)`).

For the plain named colors used by `.red` specifically, the codes come from the `styles.color` table defined earlier in the same file:

```js
color: {
	black: [30, 39],
	red: [31, 39],
	...
}
```

and are turned into open/close escape strings inside `assembleStyles()`'s main loop:

```js
for (const [groupName, group] of Object.entries(styles)) {
	for (const [styleName, style] of Object.entries(group)) {
		styles[styleName] = {
			open: `[${style[0]}m`,
			close: `[${style[1]}m`,
		};
		group[styleName] = styles[styleName];
		...
	}
	...
}
```

So `ansiStyles.color.red === {open: '[31m', close: '[39m'}`. Back in `source/index.js`, the `for (const [styleName, style] of Object.entries(ansiStyles)) { styles[styleName] = { get() { ... createStyler(style.open, style.close, ...) ... } } }` loop is what wires `chalk.red` to this `open`/`close` pair — these are the literal strings `applyStyle` concatenates around the input string when `level > 0`.

## 4. How `supports-color` computes the default level with no options (`source/vendor/supports-color/index.js`)

At module load, with no options passed by the caller:

```js
const supportsColor = {
	stdout: createSupportsColor({isTTY: tty.isatty(1)}),
	stderr: createSupportsColor({isTTY: tty.isatty(2)}),
};
```

`createSupportsColor(stream, options = {})` calls the internal detector and translates the numeric level:

```js
export function createSupportsColor(stream, options = {}) {
	const level = _supportsColor(stream, {
		streamIsTTY: stream && stream.isTTY,
		...options,
	});
	return translateLevel(level);
}
```

`_supportsColor` runs a cascade of environment/flag checks (CLI flags like `--color`/`--no-color`, `FORCE_COLOR` env var, CI environment detection, Windows build number, `TERM`, `COLORTERM`, `TERM_PROGRAM`, etc.) and returns an integer 0–3, defaulting to `min` (0 unless `FORCE_COLOR` set a floor) if nothing matches:

```js
function _supportsColor(haveStream, {streamIsTTY, sniffFlags = true} = {}) {
	...
	if (haveStream && !streamIsTTY && forceColor === undefined) {
		return 0;
	}
	const min = forceColor || 0;
	...
	return min; // fallback path
}
```

That integer level is passed through `translateLevel`:

```js
function translateLevel(level) {
	if (level === 0) {
		return false;
	}
	return {
		level,
		hasBasic: true,
		has256: level >= 2,
		has16m: level >= 3,
	};
}
```

If `level === 0`, `translateLevel` returns `false` (not an object). This is why in `source/index.js`, `applyOptions` guards with `stdoutColor ? stdoutColor.level : 0` — `stdoutColor` (i.e. `supportsColor.stdout`) may be `false`, and `false.level` would throw, so it falls back to `0` directly. This is the mechanism that determines `chalk`'s (the default export's) level when `Chalk` is constructed with no `level` option at all — it is irrelevant to `new Chalk({level: 3})` and `new Chalk({level: 0})` themselves, since those calls pass `level` explicitly and `applyOptions` uses `options.level` directly instead of consulting `stdoutColor`.

## 5. How `source/utilities.js` prevents ANSI codes leaking across an embedded newline

In `applyStyle` (`source/index.js`), after the color codes are computed, a line-break check runs:

```js
const lfIndex = string.indexOf('\n');
if (lfIndex !== -1) {
	string = stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex(string, closeAll, openAll, lfIndex);
}

return openAll + string + closeAll;
```

`stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex` in `source/utilities.js` walks every `\n` in the string (starting from the first found index) and inserts the style's `closeAll` code immediately before each line break and `openAll` immediately after it (handling `\r\n` specially so the closing code goes before the `\r`, not between `\r` and `\n`):

```js
export function stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex(string, prefix, postfix, index) {
	let endIndex = 0;
	let returnValue = '';
	do {
		const gotCR = string[index - 1] === '\r';
		returnValue += string.slice(endIndex, (gotCR ? index - 1 : index)) + prefix + (gotCR ? '\r\n' : '\n') + postfix;
		endIndex = index + 1;
		index = string.indexOf('\n', endIndex);
	} while (index !== -1);

	returnValue += string.slice(endIndex);
	return returnValue;
}
```

Here `prefix` is `closeAll` and `postfix` is `openAll`. So each line gets its own self-contained open/close pair instead of one open code at the very start and one close code at the very end spanning multiple lines — this works around a rendering bug where some terminals (macOS Terminal.app) don't carry style state across a bare linebreak, referenced in the source comment: "Close the styling before a linebreak and reopen after next line to fix a bleed issue on macOS: https://github.com/chalk/chalk/pull/92". This is confirmed by the test `'line breaks should open and close colors'` in `test/chalk.js`:

```js
test('line breaks should open and close colors', t => {
	t.is(chalk.grey('hello\nworld'), '[90mhello[39m\n[90mworld[39m');
});
```

Note this whole code path — and thus `stringEncaseCRLFWithFirstIndex` — is only reached when `self.level > 0`; at level 0 `applyStyle` returns early (see §1) before the newline-splitting logic ever runs, so the plain string's embedded newline is untouched.

## 6. The existing test pinning level-0 plain-string behavior (`test/chalk.js`)

```js
test('don\'t emit RGB codes if level is 0', t => {
	t.is(new Chalk({level: 0}).hex('#FF0000')('hello'), 'hello');
	t.is(new Chalk({level: 0}).bgHex('#FF0000')('hello'), 'hello');
});
```

This test directly constructs `new Chalk({level: 0})` and asserts the styled call returns the plain, uncolored string (`'hello'`), exercising the exact `self.level <= 0` branch in `applyStyle` described in §1. It uses `.hex(...)` rather than `.red(...)`, but both go through the same `applyStyle` gate, so it pins the same level-0 short-circuit that makes `new Chalk({level: 0}).red("foo")` return the plain `"foo"`.

